.he "paste (1)                    4/6/83                     paste (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
paste
- print files side by side
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
paste [-t separator] file1 file2 ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Paste
reads a line from each file in the order given and pastes
the lines together into a single line, which is written to the
standard output.  A tab separator is used unless another
character is specified.  A file name of - refers to the standard
input.  No attempt is made to line up the columns vertically.

There is no limit on the input line length.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
Suppose that file1 contains

.nf
.in +5
Sally
Samantha
Holly
Agatha
.in -5
.fi

and file2 contains

.nf
.in +5
Mike
Mordred
Garth
.in -5
.fi

.in +5
.bd
paste -t+ file1 file2
.in -5

would result in the following output

.nf
.in +5
Sally+Mike
Samantha+Mordred
Holly+Garth
Agatha
.in -5
.fi

.in +5
.bd
ls | paste -
.in -5

would give a two-column listing of the current directory
(alphabetized from left to right, not top to bottom).
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS AND CAVEATS
.in +5
.br
No attempt is made to align the columns vertically, except to
put tabs between the columns.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
